<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What's Your Favorite Food? – Lesson # 2</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's Your Favorite Food?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of texts by answering questions, both written and oral. Reading: Write responses to questions about a text. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show understanding of food preference texts by writing short responses (e.g., "What is his favorite food?" "He likes apples."). Can answer questions in writing about food choices based on short texts. (e.g., "What is his favorite food?" "He likes Sancocho."). Can combine individual phonemes to construct familiar one-syllable or two-syllable words, using prior knowledge of the words (e.g., blending /ˈæ-p-əl/ to form the word "apple").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays large flashcards of core food vocabulary (apple, banana, sandwich, rice with chicken, soup, juice, water). The teacher holds up each flashcard and asks students, "What food is this?" After students identify the food, the teacher asks, "Do you like [apple]?" Students respond "Yes, I do" (thumbs up) or "No, I don't" (thumbs down) chorally and individually, activating prior knowledge of food vocabulary and simple preferences.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language: "I like apples." "I don't like soup." The teacher also models the pronunciation of "favorite" (/ˈfeɪvərɪt/) and "snack" (/snæk/) by saying the words clearly and having students repeat. For phonemic awareness, the teacher models blending by clearly articulating phonemes and then the full word: "/æ-p-əl/ - apple," and "/w-ɔːt-ər/ - water," encouraging student repetition.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I like juice,' do I want to drink juice or not drink juice?" (Expected Answer: Drink juice.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I show you a picture of a sandwich and ask 'Do you like sandwiches?', what are two ways you can answer?" (Expected Answer: Yes, I do. / No, I don't.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>0-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated short text titled "My Favorite Foods" displayed on the board or as a handout. The text describes a child's food preferences for different meals and snacks. It is a short narrative excerpt with simple sentences, targeting an A1.1 reading level.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text:</i><br>Hi! My name is Leo. I like to eat healthy food. For breakfast, I like bananas and juice. For lunch, my favorite food is rice with chicken. I like rice with chicken, but I don't like soup. For my snack, I like an apple. My friend Mia likes apples, but she doesn't like bananas.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of texts by answering questions, both written and oral.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher asks, "Who is the boy in the story?" and "What is the story about?" Students provide short oral answers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher reads statements about the text (e.g., "Leo likes soup." "Leo likes bananas." "Mia likes juice.") and students indicate True or False by showing a green card for True and a red card for False.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students draw lines to match food items from the text to the correct meal times (e.g., banana – breakfast; rice with chicken – lunch; apple – snack).</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>7-17 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of short texts about food preferences, focusing on identifying specific information and answering questions. Grammar: WH- and yes/no questions, Present simple, Compound sentences with 'but'. Vocabulary: apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup. Pronunciation: /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack". Phonemic awareness: blending of spoken phonemes to form one-syllable and two-syllable familiar words related to food (e.g., /ˈæ-p.əl/, /ˈw-ɔːt-ər/).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap Fill):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the "My Favorite Foods" text again, but with certain key food words (e.g., banana, juice, rice, chicken, soup, apple) replaced by blanks. Students read the text carefully and choose words from a provided word bank to fill in the missing information, ensuring they use the target vocabulary accurately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Question Answering):</b> The teacher displays several WH- and Yes/No questions about the text (e.g., "What is Leo's favorite food for lunch?", "Does Leo like soup?", "What does Mia like for a snack?") on the board. Students read the questions individually and write short, complete answers in their notebooks, referencing the text directly to ensure accuracy.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Peer Check:</b> The teacher orally presents segmented phonemes for "apple" (/æ-p-əl/) and "water" (/w-ɔːt-ər/). Students practice blending these phonemes to form the words. Then, in pairs, students take turns reading a sentence from the text that contains words with /f/ (favorite) and /s/ (snack) sounds, pointing to these words. They then orally ask each other one or two questions from Activity 2 and check if their partner's written answers match the text.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>17-30 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Time to Eat."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Read and Respond to a Friend's Favorite Food" – Students will read a short text about a friend's food preferences and write answers to comprehension questions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Each student receives a "Food Preference Card" containing a short, illustrated paragraph (2-3 sentences) about an imaginary friend's food preferences, using similar vocabulary and grammatical structures as the "My Favorite Foods" text. For example: "This is Sofia. She likes fruit for breakfast. Her favorite snack is a banana. She likes water, but she doesn't like juice." Students read their assigned card individually. After reading, they write short responses to 2-3 specific comprehension questions about the friend on their card (e.g., "What is Sofia's favorite food for breakfast?", "Does Sofia like juice?"), demonstrating their reading comprehension by extracting and writing the correct information.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>30-40 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> The teacher will use an observation checklist during the production task to assess individual students' ability to accurately read the preference cards and write correct short responses. Additionally, the written answers from the assessment task will be collected and reviewed.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a "Food Quiz" worksheet. This worksheet contains a new, short (3-4 sentences) illustrated text about another character's food preferences. Below the text, there are two WH- questions (e.g., "What is his favorite food for dinner?") and one Yes/No question (e.g., "Does he like soup?"). Students read the text independently and write their answers on the worksheet.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate comprehension: Student extracts key information from the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct answers: Written responses accurately reflect the information in the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary: Uses appropriate food vocabulary (e.g., apple, banana, juice, water, sandwich, rice with chicken).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Uses simple present tense and basic sentence structures (e.g., "He likes...", "No, he doesn't.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic Awareness (Oral Check): Blends phonemes correctly when asked to orally pronounce familiar words from the text (e.g., "apple", "water").</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on an index card before leaving:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing that was easy for me to read today was..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One word I want to learn more about is..."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>40-45 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review, self-assessment, and forward planning.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn today about reading texts about food preferences?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I practice reading more English words about food at home?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully identify main ideas and details in the various short texts? Were the texts adequately challenging but not overwhelming for their reading level (A1.1)?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the phonemic awareness activities effective for blending one and two-syllable words? Do I need to provide more explicit support for specific sounds like /f/ and /s/ or blending patterns in the next lesson?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking) to ensure students can confidently use the vocabulary and grammar to express and ask about food preferences?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will actively *speak* about our own and others' favorite foods. We will use the vocabulary and questions we practiced reading today to ask and answer 'What's your favorite food?' and 'Do you like...?' in interactive pairs."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1<br>45-50 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>